      "content": "Indeed, cultural habits and hygiene practices should be factored into the model to better predict the virus spread in District 2. For example, community events where close contact is common could escalate transmission, especially if gatherings occur without the adoption of traditional mask-wearing or physical distancing. Cultural norms also play a significant role, as congregate living situations common among lower-income immigrant groups may lack the necessary ventilation systems or space to adequately limit contact. Household sizes also influence transmission rates; larger households can struggle with more extended transmission chains. If possible, collect data on the frequency and nature of social interactions within these communities, along with subjective reports on hygiene practices known to reduce the spread of respiratory illnesses. Conducting a sensitivity analysis with these additional factors would enhance predictive accuracy for outbreak hotspots. Engaging with community leaders and public health professionals who speak the languages in question can help ensure effective communication and intervention strategies are designed and implemented.",
